# Copied to `.env` (git-ignored) for the E2E suite only — the app itself never
# reads this file. Specs that need something from here skip without it.

# ── A live model ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `make e2e-live` (live.spec.ts). One real model turn.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# ── A live cinna-core ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `make e2e-integration` (cinna-integration.spec.ts): the desktop, cinna-core
# and cinna-cli driven together against a server actually running on this
# machine. Everything below is empty by default and those specs skip.
#
# CINNA_E2E_SERVER_URL is the origin the desktop is pointed at — the one that
# serves `/.well-known/cinna-desktop`, i.e. the **backend**, not the SPA. For a
# local stack that is usually http://localhost:8000, not the Vite dev server on
# :5173.
#
# The discovery document's own `authorization_endpoint` has to be reachable
# from this machine too: cinna-core builds it from its `BACKEND_BASE_URL`, so a
# server configured to advertise a tunnel that is down will fail the spec's
# preflight with that endpoint named. Point `BACKEND_BASE_URL` at the same
# localhost origin for local integration runs.
#
# The credentials are a normal account on that instance, used only to approve
# the desktop's authorization the way the user's browser would. For local
# development this is typically cinna-core's FIRST_SUPERUSER.
#
# The account needs the `agent-developer` or `admin` role for the local-dev half
# of the run; without it the spec still asserts the connect flow and records
# `unsupported/role`, which is a real, supported outcome rather than a failure.
CINNA_E2E_SERVER_URL=
CINNA_E2E_EMAIL=
CINNA_E2E_PASSWORD=
